The format of Hardware/parts.txt
================================

The list of parts worth using again: JLCPCB part numbers, packages, and
how JLCPCB's footprint for a part differs from ours.  Read by people
scanning for a part, and by `jlc`, which checks the KiCad projects
against it and builds the BOM's package column out of it.

Each entry is an index line ending in ':', then indented body lines.
The index is what a person would call the part: the value or family
first, the package last, whatever distinguishes it in between.  For
resistors and capacitors that includes the voltage rating and usually
the tolerance, so the choice is explicit.  Index lines are unique, which
is what catches a part being listed twice under two numbers.

Body lines are recognised by shape, not by tags:

	Cnnnnnn [basic|promo]	the JLCPCB part, and its pricing tier
	MPN: <part number>	the manufacturer's own, spelled out
	http... or www...	a datasheet
	CHECK: <sentence>	look at this before placing an order
	JLC: rotate <deg>	JLCPCB's footprint against ours
	JLC: offset <dx> <dy>	   - see below
	one word with a ':'	the KiCad footprint
	anything else		manufacturer and part number

CHECK: is tagged because prose has no shape that tells it apart from the
manufacturer line.  Every one is reprinted, for every part in a board's
BOM, when the manufacturing files are made - so it is for what gets
checked by eye and cannot be checked by anything else.  Keep it to the
instruction.

MPN: is only needed when the part number is not the last word of the
manufacturer line, because that is what `jlc sync` writes otherwise. One
entry needs it: SHOU HAN's `TYPE-C 16P CB1.6 073`, whose number is four
words and whose maker's name is two. Without the line, sync silently
rewrites a correct part number in the schematic to `073`.

JLC: is how JLCPCB's own footprint differs from ours, measured from
EasyEDA's data rather than remembered.  Both numbers are in *our*
footprint's frame:

	rotate D	their drawing turns by D to land on ours
	offset dx dy	their origin sits here in our frame

and the position file is corrected by them automatically.  No JLC: line
means no correction *that a footprint can show* - rotation also depends
on how the part sits in its tape, which is in no file either side has,
so these never retire the CHECK: ones.

The footprint is written only when it cannot be derived.  A resistor or
capacitor with an imperial size in its index gets the obvious KiCad name
from the section heading it sits under, so the ## headings are load
bearing.  Inductors are not derivable - two 0806 parts here have
different footprints - and neither is anything bespoke.

Package names are JLCPCB's where those are clean and trimmed where they
are not: SOT-666 rather than SOT-666-6, QFN-16 rather than QFN-16-EP(3x3).

Entries are sorted within a section: by value for the passives, by name
for everything else.

Comments
--------

One line, or none.  A comment earns its line only when it is critical to
the part and is not already in the index line above it - a polarised
inductor, an absolute maximum, which of two suffixes means what.

Not: how the part was used on some board, that it has been verified,
that it differs from a part it obviously differs from, or what happened
on a past order.  That is the schematic's job, or the commit's, and here
it hides the entries and the few comments that matter.

Reading the data
----------------

Picking a part often means searching JLCPCB, sorting by stock, and
taking something near the top; stock is a real criterion and sometimes
the deciding one.  So a tight tolerance or an unusual dielectric in an
entry is not automatically a requirement.  Where a spec is load bearing,
the comment says so.

'basic' is JLCPCB's Basic part, which costs no setup fee, and 'promo' is
Promotional Extended.  Both change over time and neither is checked.

'TODO' marks an entry carrying only what the schematics knew.
